$title="Horse Racing’s Most Important Days of the Year";
$content='<p>There are few more active sports than horse racing throughout the year. Events on track are staged virtually every day across the United States, Europe and Australia.</p>
<p>In fact, there is also racing to enjoy on Christmas Day in Japan. However, not every day of racing is as important as the rest, as there are some notable events that capture the mainstream attention due to the cultural impact of the contests and mega money that could be on offer.</p>
<p><img alt="horse racings" src="https://www.dreamcalendars.com/images/horse-racings-most-important-days-of-the-year.jpg" class="img-fluid" /></p>
<p>Some races have the power to shut down an entire country, with millions hooked to the action taking place on the track. However, what are some of the most important race days on the international schedule according to the experts at <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.twinspires.com/edge/racing/wagering/best-bets/">TwinSpires</a>?</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Dubai World Cup</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>One of the first big prizes for international flat stars is staged in March, as the G1 Dubai World Cup is held at Meydan Racecourse in the United Arab Emirates.</p>
<p>Despite lacking the prestige of other major races on the flat schedule, a mega purse of $12 million is up for grabs in the Dubai World Cup, meaning that international superstars typically travel to the Middle East for the race that is run over around ten furlongs.</p>
<p>There was early evidence of that in the inaugural edition in 1996 when Hall of Famer Cigar landing victory. Other notable winners of the race include Street Cry, Curlin, and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2016/mar/26/california-chrome-stunning-win-meydan-dubai-world-cup-horse-racing">California Chrome</a>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Kentucky Derby</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The standout race in the first half of a calendar year in the United States comes in the form of the Kentucky Derby. Churchill Downs hosts the major event on the first Saturday in May, which forms the opening leg of the Triple Crown.</p>
<p>The race is competed over a distance of 1 ¼ miles on the dirt, with the winner being covered in a bed or roses. It is regularly the most attended and watched race in North America, and it has been home to some of the most defining images of American racing.</p>
<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.racingmuseum.org/hall-of-fame/horse/secretariat-va">Secretariat remains one of the most iconic winners</a>, and the Hall of the Famer continues to hold the speed record.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Epsom Derby</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>While the Kentucky Derby is most famous in North America, the inspiration for the U.S. race was taken from the Epsom Derby, which is held annually in June in the United Kingdom.</p>
<p>The race is one of the most important races in Europe, as there is a direct impact on the industry when it comes to stud costs. The Derby was first staged in 1780, and it is held annually at Epsom.</p>
<p>Horses aged three will compete over a mile and four furlongs, and it serves as the middle leg of the British Triple Crown, following the 2,000 Guineas and before the St Leger. However, it is extremely rare for a Derby runner to target Triple Crown success in this modern era.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Cheltenham Gold Cup</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Despite the Derby being the most important race in the UK, it could be easily argued that the race that the entire country looks forward to is the Cheltenham Gold Cup.</p>
<p>The popularity of jumps racing exceeds that of the flat across Britain and Ireland, and the Gold Cup serves as the highlight of the four-day Cheltenham Festival, which regularly attracts over 70,000 people per day.</p>
<p>The Blue Riband event on the jumps schedule is staged annually in March, with the field competing over a distance of three miles and two furlongs, with 22 fences being jumped. This race was first contested in 1924, and it has been won by some of the most iconic horses on the National Hunt schedule, including Denman, Kauto Star and Arkle.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Breeders’ Cup Classic</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Intriguing championship races are staged throughout the second half of the year across the world, and few are as watched in North America than the Breeders’ Cup Classic.</p>
<p>This race rounds off the prestigious two-day meet in the United States, and it is considered to be the premier thoroughbred race of the season in America.</p>
<p>It is the fourth leg of the Grand Slam of Racing, with only American Pharoah completing the set after winning the Classic in 2015 following victories in the Kentucky Derby, Preakness, and Belmont Stakes.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Melbourne Cup</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In Australia, the Melbourne Cup is the race that has the power to stop the entire nation. The Grade One is held annually at Flemington Racecourse in Melbourne, and it has been run outside of War time in every year since 1861.</p>
<p>The Melbourne Cup is one of the world’s richest turf races, and it is the most lucrative two-mile handicap in the world with an annual purse of A$8.4 million being on offer.</p>
<p>There have been legendary winners of the race throughout history, with the most successful being Makybe Diva, who won three successive editions of the race between 2003 and 2005.&nbsp;<br>&nbsp;</p>
